WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.760 --> 00:00:07.920
After months of exhausting siege warfare, two of&nbsp;
Europe’s leading powers—the Kingdom of France and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:14.240
the Habsburgs—clashed at the Battle of Pavia.&nbsp;
The battle marked the dramatic climax of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:19.200
decades-long struggle for supremacy on&nbsp;
the continent, fought primarily on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:27.120
battlefields of Italy. It was also a pivotal&nbsp;
moment in military development. At Pavia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:32.800
the modern pike-and-shot infantry of Emperor&nbsp;
Charles V faced the most formidable heavy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:40.080
cavalry in Europe—the French gendarmes.&nbsp;
Would the old elite prevail once more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:46.800
or was a new era about to begin? This&nbsp;
is how modern historiography recounts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:56.960
the Battle of Pavia.
Chapter 1: Hegemony&nbsp;

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:02.720
In the early 16th century, two powerful&nbsp;
dynasties vied for supremacy in Europe:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:10.160
the French royal house of Valois and the Habsburg&nbsp;
dynasty. Their rivalry intensified when two young&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:19.440
and ambitious rulers came to power—Francis I of&nbsp;
Valois and Charles V of Habsburg. When Charles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:26.800
King of Spain at the time, was elected King&nbsp;
of the Romans in 1519, uniting his kingdom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:33.040
and the Holy Roman Empire under his rule,&nbsp;
the balance of power shifted dramatically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:40.400
in favor of the Habsburgs. France suddenly&nbsp;
found itself surrounded by hostile powers.&nbsp;

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:46.320
The French king’s worst fears were realized&nbsp;
when Charles V’s troops invaded the Duchy of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:55.040
Milan in the autumn of 1521—a region that had long&nbsp;
served as a southern buffer zone for France. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.040 --> 00:02:02.320
commander of the French forces in Italy, Odet de&nbsp;
Foix, Viscount de Lautrec, did his utmost to halt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:10.320
the imperial advance, but after a crushing defeat&nbsp;
at the Battle of Bicocca on April 27, 1522, he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:19.600
forced to evacuate Lombardy. From that moment&nbsp;
on, Francis I was determined to recover Milan.&nbsp;

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:27.360
A favorable opportunity arose two years later.&nbsp;
In July 1524, Charles III, Duke of Bourbon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:33.680
led a campaign into Provence on behalf of&nbsp;
the emperor. His army marched west along the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:40.480
Mediterranean coast, overrunning one city after&nbsp;
another until only Marseille remained in French&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:47.440
hands. Then the advance came to a halt. The walls&nbsp;
of the port city withstood Bourbon’s artillery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:55.200
until King Francis I marched to Avignon and forced&nbsp;
him to retreat. The withdrawal quickly turned into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.200 --> 00:03:02.720
a rout, and the imperial forces abandoned much of&nbsp;
their heavy equipment in their haste, even casting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:09.440
their cannons into the Mediterranean to facilitate&nbsp;
their escape. By early October, they had finally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:17.200
returned to Italy, which they believed was safe.
However, King Francis I was not only pursuing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:22.480
Bourbon to drive him out of Provence, but&nbsp;
also because he saw an opportunity to recover&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:28.000
Lombardy. His army clearly outnumbered&nbsp;
the imperial forces and was in far better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:35.120
condition. To maintain momentum while in pursuit,&nbsp;
Francis divided his army into three columns:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:43.760
the Connétable of France, Anne de Montmorency,&nbsp;
continued the chase; Jacques II de Chabannes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:51.520
Seigneur de La Palice, advanced toward Italy via&nbsp;
Asti; and Francis himself moved over the Susa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:58.400
Pass to Turin to meet 14,000 mercenaries&nbsp;
sent there by the Old Swiss Confederacy.&nbsp;

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:05.440
At a later point, he was also joined by one of the&nbsp;
most respected mercenary companies in Italy—the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:13.280
Bande Nere or Black Bands—whose leader, Giovanni&nbsp;
de’ Medici, switched sides after a dispute over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:21.680
pay. When La Palice rejoined the king near&nbsp;
Novara, heavy clouds darkened the sky. Francis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:32.720
now commanded nearly 40,000 men—twice the strength&nbsp;
of his opponents. A storm was brewing over Italy.&nbsp;

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:39.920
The rains came at the end of October. Francis&nbsp;
marched on Milan, where Charles de Lannoy, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:46.080
imperial commander-in-chief in Italy, had&nbsp;
entrenched himself with around 16,000 men.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:52.320
As the city came into view, a dripping&nbsp;
wet delegation rode out to meet the army.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:57.680
To everyone’s astonishment, they humbly&nbsp;
offered to surrender and requested that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:05.600
a French garrison be installed. Francis was taken&nbsp;
aback, but he accepted. As he entered the city on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:15.600
October 26, however, he understood why Lannoy&nbsp;
had withdrawn: the plague was raging in Milan.&nbsp;

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:22.960
Francis had just missed Lannoy, who had decided&nbsp;
that there was no point in sacrificing his men&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:29.520
in the hopeless defense of a plague-ridden&nbsp;
city. Instead, he abandoned Milan to its fate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:35.760
left a strong garrison in the strategically&nbsp;
important city of Pavia, and withdrew to Lodi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:42.400
to gather reinforcements for a counterattack.
After they arrived in Milan, the French debated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:48.080
how to proceed. As long as the imperial army&nbsp;
stayed intact, their position in Italy would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:54.480
remain uncertain. Most of Francis’s advisors&nbsp;
urged him to pursue Lannoy and engage him in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.480 --> 00:06:00.800
battle before Bourbon could join him or further&nbsp;
reinforcements could arrive. Only Guillaume de&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:08.000
Bonnivet, Admiral of France, and the Duke&nbsp;
of Alençon argued for a different approach:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:14.400
They suggested taking Pavia first to secure&nbsp;
the army’s flank. Weakened by a long march,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:20.320
heavy autumn storms, and persistent rain,&nbsp;
Francis chose the seemingly more cautious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:32.960
course.INF He marched toward Pavia.
Chapter 2: The Siege of Pavia&nbsp;

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:39.040
When the guards spotted the first French&nbsp;
banners on the horizon on October 28, 1524,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.040 --> 00:06:46.400
the rain was still falling in dense sheets.&nbsp;
Moments later, the bells sounded the alarm.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:53.120
The garrison rushed to their posts, and onlookers&nbsp;
gathered along the walls. From there, they watched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.120 --> 00:07:00.080
as the French encircled Pavia, drawing an&nbsp;
ever-closer ring around the city. Soon their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:06.720
field fortifications stretched from the Visconti&nbsp;
Park —a walled hunting ground to the north—to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:14.320
Ticino River in the south. A few hours later,&nbsp;
the deep rumble of the cannons reverberated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:23.200
through the rain-soaked air. The siege had begun.
Pavia was a force to be reckoned with. Situated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:30.320
on the Ticino and enclosed by walls built to the&nbsp;
latest Italian standards, Antonio de Leyva was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:36.000
prepared to defend it with 9,000 mercenaries.&nbsp;
The veteran commander had sufficient supplies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:43.040
to hold out well into the spring—only funds were&nbsp;
in short supply. As the fight dragged on, he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:48.880
famously forced to confiscate and melt down the&nbsp;
church’s liturgical equipment to pay the garrison.&nbsp;

00:07:50.080 --> 00:07:57.600
The siege began with an artillery duel, but this&nbsp;
soon ceased as the rain turned into a deluge.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:04.160
Only after two weeks, when the downpour finally&nbsp;
subsided, did the French bombardment reach full&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:11.120
force. From then on, cannon fire thundered&nbsp;
incessantly against Pavia’s walls until,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:18.880
by mid-November, two breaches had been forced&nbsp;
into the city walls. Hoping to bring matters to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.880 --> 00:08:26.400
a swift conclusion, Francis ordered an assault.
The French infantry rushed at both breaches&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:33.360
at once. Under a hail of gunfire, the soldiers&nbsp;
fought their way forward, stone splintering and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:39.600
earth spraying into the air as they laboriously&nbsp;
climbed the rubble toward the gaps in the wall.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:45.760
Once at the top, they surged forward with a war&nbsp;
cry, throwing themselves at the defenders who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.760 --> 00:08:54.080
were waiting for them in the breach. Then steel&nbsp;
met steel. Swords clanged, axes shattered bracers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.080 --> 00:09:00.960
and greaves, and maces smashed into helmeted&nbsp;
heads. The fighting surged back and forth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:07.520
within the narrow openings until the garrison&nbsp;
finally began to give ground. The French cheered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.520 --> 00:09:14.000
and pressed forward in pursuit of what seemed&nbsp;
to be a retreating enemy—until a sudden hail&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:20.160
of bullets tore through their ranks. Behind the&nbsp;
rampart, the defenders had prepared a kill zone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:26.480
where hundreds of arquebusiers stood ready behind&nbsp;
improvised earthen breastworks. Their volley&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.480 --> 00:09:35.360
drove the French back out of the fortress within&nbsp;
minutes, shattering any hope of a swift victory.&nbsp;

00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:41.920
After this defeat, morale in the French camp&nbsp;
collapsed. The rain had turned the plateau around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.920 --> 00:09:49.120
Pavia into a swamp; the soldiers were soaked&nbsp;
to the bone, and the siege ground to a halt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.120 --> 00:09:56.720
as the gunpowder became moist and the artillery&nbsp;
was forced to pause its firing several times. To&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.720 --> 00:10:03.760
avoid waiting idly for better weather, the French&nbsp;
turned to an alternative plan: they attempted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.760 --> 00:10:11.520
to build a dam upstream of Pavia to divert the&nbsp;
Ticino. Hundreds of laborers set to work, but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:18.480
swollen river proved too powerful, and the current&nbsp;
washed the dam away before it could be completed.&nbsp;

00:10:18.480 --> 00:10:23.520
After this failure, increased frustration&nbsp;
spread through the French camp, and many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:30.480
urged the king to abandon the siege and return&nbsp;
home. The stalemate showed no sign of breaking:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:37.440
the garrison had not revealed any weakness, and&nbsp;
soon the rain would turn into snow. With each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.440 --> 00:10:44.960
passing day spent freezing outside the city, the&nbsp;
French grew weaker—while Lannoy’s relief army grew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.960 --> 00:10:52.880
stronger. Yet despite the persistent warnings of&nbsp;
his generals, Francis was not ready to give up.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:59.200
Instead of withdrawing, he attempted to divert&nbsp;
the imperial forces by sending the Marquis of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.200 --> 00:11:06.240
Saluzzo to Liguria and ordering the Duke of&nbsp;
Albany to lead a campaign against Naples.&nbsp;

00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:12.880
While Saluzzo successfully placed pressure on&nbsp;
Genoa, the campaign against Naples faltered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:19.760
before it had truly begun. Albany managed&nbsp;
to repel an attack by Lannoy at Fiorenzuola,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.760 --> 00:11:25.600
but a sudden cold snap forced him to halt&nbsp;
his advance. In the improvised winter camp,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.600 --> 00:11:32.400
his soldiers were exposed to wind and weather, and&nbsp;
desertion and illness quickly thinned their ranks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.400 --> 00:11:40.000
melting his army away like butter in a hot&nbsp;
pan. When the winter frost finally abated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:45.200
Albany barely made it to the Tiber before&nbsp;
he abandoned the campaign and led the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:52.160
broken remnants of his force back to France.
In the meantime, the Duke of Bourbon and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.160 --> 00:12:00.320
famous Georg von Frundsberg had traveled to&nbsp;
Austria to recruit reinforcements. Frundsberg&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.320 --> 00:12:05.840
was a legend among the Landsknechts. His&nbsp;
reputation—and the substantial financial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.840 --> 00:12:12.800
backing of the Pope, the emperor, and wealthy&nbsp;
investors—drew around 15,000 mercenaries within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.800 --> 00:12:18.320
a remarkably short time. At the end of the&nbsp;
year, they set out across the snow-covered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.320 --> 00:12:24.800
Alps. Together with several hundred men-at-arms&nbsp;
from the Netherlands, they reached the army camp&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.800 --> 00:12:31.840
near Lodi on January 10, 1525. With these&nbsp;
reinforcements, the imperial forces were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.840 --> 00:12:48.536
now strong enough to take the offensive.&nbsp;
Two weeks later, Lannoy set out for Pavia.&nbsp;

00:12:48.536 --> 00:12:48.626
At this point, let us take a short break for&nbsp;
today’s reading tip: the novel The Scarlet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.626 --> 00:12:48.718
City by Hella S. Haasse. In this captivating&nbsp;
story, the bestselling Dutch author transports&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.718 --> 00:12:48.811
her readers into the world of the powerful&nbsp;
Borgia family—a world of intrigue, power games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.811 --> 00:12:48.904
and decadence—and traces the true origins of&nbsp;
Giovanni Borgia, who is believed to have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.904 --> 00:12:49.004
an illegitimate son of Pope Alexander VI. You can&nbsp;
find a link to the novel in the description below.&nbsp;

00:12:49.004 --> 00:12:51.840
Chapter 3: From Besieger to Besieged
When news of the counter-offensive arrived,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.840 --> 00:12:58.080
King Francis reorganized his troops, moved the&nbsp;
main camp to the northeast of the Visconti Park,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.080 --> 00:13:04.640
and ordered his army to dig in. The soldiers&nbsp;
laboriously broke up the frozen ground,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.640 --> 00:13:10.400
erected fortified positions, and included the&nbsp;
park in their defensive perimeter by extending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.400 --> 00:13:16.560
its walls with trenches and field fortifications&nbsp;
all the way to the banks of the Ticino.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.560 --> 00:13:24.480
In doing so, Francis built himself a fortress—and&nbsp;
transformed from besieger to besieged.&nbsp;

00:13:24.480 --> 00:13:30.320
The fact that the king allowed himself to be&nbsp;
pinned down outside Pavia with an exhausted army&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.320 --> 00:13:38.000
after the harsh winter shocked many Frenchmen. In&nbsp;
doing so, he accepted another weeks-long war of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.000 --> 00:13:43.920
attrition and relinquished any real opportunity&nbsp;
to actively shape the course of the campaign.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.480 --> 00:13:50.960
This choice was especially surprising in the&nbsp;
case of Francis I, who had a reputation as an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.960 --> 00:13:58.080
astute tactician and was known for his ability&nbsp;
to impose his rhythm on the enemy. The king’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.080 --> 00:14:03.600
decision remains a matter of debate to this&nbsp;
day. Some speculate that he was suffering from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.600 --> 00:14:10.000
the symptoms of syphilis, which he is believed&nbsp;
to have contracted shortly before the siege,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:15.440
while others argue that he was simply&nbsp;
convinced he could outlast the garrison.&nbsp;

00:14:15.440 --> 00:14:24.320
The imperial army arrived in Lardirago on February&nbsp;
2, 1525. There, Lannoy assembled his troops and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.320 --> 00:14:30.480
waited for the Marquis of Pescara, who, with the&nbsp;
rearguard, formed the tail end of the long column.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.120 --> 00:14:36.720
Meanwhile, Bourbon rode ahead, establishing&nbsp;
a fortified camp and two artillery batteries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.720 --> 00:14:42.640
at the Casa dei Levrieri, where the new&nbsp;
French lines met the Visconti Park. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.640 --> 00:14:47.360
then informed Lannoy that the rest of the&nbsp;
army could advance and opened a bombardment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.360 --> 00:14:53.440
against the French defenses. The shelling&nbsp;
caused little damage, but it signaled to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.440 --> 00:15:00.400
garrison that relief had finally arrived.
After the arrival of the imperial troops,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.400 --> 00:15:08.720
a routine quickly took hold: while the batteries&nbsp;
at Torre del Gallo exchanged monotonous volleys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.720 --> 00:15:15.920
both sides continued to expand their positions and&nbsp;
probe the enemy. Yet neither side made any real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.920 --> 00:15:23.440
progress. At the same time, frustration grew:&nbsp;
the imperial soldiers’ pay was long overdue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.440 --> 00:15:29.120
and there was little incentive to stay put&nbsp;
and wait for the money. French bullets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.120 --> 00:15:35.760
snow, and rain battered the men as they sat&nbsp;
in wet, muddy trenches—freezing, starving,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.760 --> 00:15:43.360
and dying. Frundsberg did everything he could&nbsp;
to motivate his Landsknechts, but to no avail.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.360 --> 00:15:50.160
Neither his rousing speeches nor the gallows that&nbsp;
stood ominously at the edge of the camp had any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.160 --> 00:15:58.880
lasting effect. Casualties mounted, and desertions&nbsp;
kept apace. When, in mid-February, a mud-spattered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.880 --> 00:16:05.600
messenger from the city burst into the imperial&nbsp;
camp and reported that the garrison too was on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.600 --> 00:16:12.560
verge of mutiny and had almost exhausted its&nbsp;
supply of powder, Lannoy knew he had to act.&nbsp;

00:16:13.520 --> 00:16:19.520
On February 21, the imperial commander&nbsp;
convened a council of war. He explained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.520 --> 00:16:26.000
to those present that they had no more than&nbsp;
four days to force a decision; otherwise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.000 --> 00:16:32.400
all their efforts would have been in vain. As&nbsp;
it was impossible to engage the French in battle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:32.400 --> 00:16:38.560
under current conditions, the council agreed on&nbsp;
an alternative: the garrison would be supplied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.560 --> 00:16:44.640
with provisions and money. The bulk of the army&nbsp;
would pin the enemy in place, while a small,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:44.640 --> 00:16:52.160
fast-moving assault force would open a corridor to&nbsp;
the city and deliver what de Leyva needed. If the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.160 --> 00:16:58.480
French king saw that the garrison was receiving&nbsp;
supplies and could hold out longer than expected,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:58.480 --> 00:17:04.960
his hopes would shatter into a thousand pieces.
The moment was favorable for such a raid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:04.960 --> 00:17:12.240
as a wave of desertions had left the French in&nbsp;
a precarious position. After Giovanni de’ Medici&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.240 --> 00:17:18.480
was seriously wounded during a sortie,&nbsp;
two-thirds of the Black Bands withdrew,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.480 --> 00:17:26.320
and just three days later, they were followed&nbsp;
by around 6,000 Swiss mercenaries. Officially,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:26.320 --> 00:17:32.960
they returned home to defend their homeland&nbsp;
against imperial raids—though in reality, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:32.960 --> 00:17:39.120
Swiss veterans likely recognized that Francis was&nbsp;
heading toward disaster, and they were unwilling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.120 --> 00:17:46.000
to sacrifice themselves in vain. The king appealed&nbsp;
to their sense of honor and reminded them of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:46.000 --> 00:17:53.360
contracts, but the Swiss merely laughed, gathered&nbsp;
their belongings, and departed. For the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:53.360 --> 00:18:01.120
time, the French found themselves outnumbered.
From the moment the war council had made its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:01.120 --> 00:18:08.160
decision, Lannoy sent his engineers out each night&nbsp;
to search for the most suitable point of attack.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:08.160 --> 00:18:14.560
The assault was to come through the park, where&nbsp;
the French would least expect it. As a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.560 --> 00:18:19.600
the engineers’ reconnaissance pushed&nbsp;
further and further north until they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:19.600 --> 00:18:27.360
reached Porta Pescarina on the night of February&nbsp;
22. While inspecting a position west of the gate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:27.360 --> 00:18:33.840
they suddenly encountered a French patrol.&nbsp;
The men ran for their lives. Three managed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:33.840 --> 00:18:41.200
escape and reported back to Lannoy that, to the&nbsp;
north, there was a spot with ground firm enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:41.200 --> 00:18:48.240
to support cavalry and artillery, and a forest&nbsp;
dense enough to provide cover yet not so thick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:48.240 --> 00:19:09.261
as to obstruct the advance. The decision was made.&nbsp;
Lannoy set the raid for the night of February 23.&nbsp;

00:19:09.261 --> 00:19:11.680
Chapter 4: The Raid
The imperial troops marched silently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:11.680 --> 00:19:18.240
northward. In the foggy night, they resembled&nbsp;
a procession of ghosts, as the men wore white&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:18.240 --> 00:19:25.520
shirts over their armor so they could recognize&nbsp;
one another in the dark. Not a word was spoken,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:25.520 --> 00:19:32.720
not a drumbeat sounded, not a trumpet was&nbsp;
blown—only the muffled crunch of heavy boots&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:32.720 --> 00:19:40.560
hitting the frozen ground and the occasional clink&nbsp;
of metal broke the silence. Apart from a few men&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:40.560 --> 00:19:48.160
manning the lines and artillery, and the guards&nbsp;
tending the campfires, everyone was on the move.&nbsp;

00:19:48.160 --> 00:19:53.920
When the first soldiers arrived at Porta&nbsp;
Pescarina, they saw the engineers laboring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:53.920 --> 00:20:01.040
to breach the wall with pickaxes and crowbars.&nbsp;
The work should have been finished long before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:01.040 --> 00:20:07.600
but the five-meter-high wall of the park proved&nbsp;
more resilient than expected. The workers had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:07.600 --> 00:20:14.160
to pry the stones out one by one, working&nbsp;
in silence so as not to alert the French.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:14.160 --> 00:20:20.960
Yet no matter how carefully they worked,&nbsp;
the effort did not go entirely unnoticed.&nbsp;

00:20:20.960 --> 00:20:26.320
Around four o’clock in the morning, a French&nbsp;
patrol heard a strange scratching and knocking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:26.320 --> 00:20:31.600
coming from the north wall of the park. The&nbsp;
riders immediately informed the commander&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:31.600 --> 00:20:38.560
of their sector, Charles Tiercelin, who set&nbsp;
off with his light cavalry to investigate. To&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:38.560 --> 00:20:45.760
be on the safe side, he requested support from&nbsp;
Robert III de La Marck, Seigneur de Fleuranges,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:45.760 --> 00:20:53.200
who promptly departed with 3,000 Swiss.
Almost an hour later, the breach was passable. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:53.200 --> 00:20:59.920
first to enter the park were 3,000 arquebusiers.&nbsp;
They formed the core of Lannoy’s plan of attack,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:59.920 --> 00:21:06.240
as they carried not only their weapons but also&nbsp;
supplies, powder, and money for the garrison.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:06.240 --> 00:21:11.680
Although they could not transport as much as&nbsp;
wagons, they were far less conspicuous and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:11.680 --> 00:21:17.440
moved much faster. To keep them concealed&nbsp;
for as long as possible, their commander,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:17.440 --> 00:21:25.680
Alfonso d’Avalos, Marquis of Vasto, immediately&nbsp;
led them into the shelter of the undergrowth.&nbsp;

00:21:25.680 --> 00:21:32.080
As soon as the arquebusiers had entered the park,&nbsp;
the engineers stepped up their efforts. No longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:32.080 --> 00:21:38.080
needing to work in complete silence, they quickly&nbsp;
widened the breach; within another hour, it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:38.080 --> 00:21:45.280
broad enough to allow artillery to pass through.&nbsp;
As the first guns slowly rolled into the park&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:45.280 --> 00:21:51.600
with groaning, creaking wheels, several hundred&nbsp;
cavalrymen swarmed out to the south to protect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:51.600 --> 00:21:59.040
them. At nearly the same time, Guillaume de&nbsp;
Bonnivet joined Tiercelin with fifty men-at-arms.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:59.040 --> 00:22:05.360
The king had sent his admiral to investigate the&nbsp;
source of the disturbance. The two rode together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:05.360 --> 00:22:12.800
toward the suspicious sounds when, suddenly, the&nbsp;
imperial cavalry emerged from the fog. The French&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:12.800 --> 00:22:19.920
charged—and in doing so, they missed the last&nbsp;
of the arquebusiers slipping into the forest.&nbsp;

00:22:19.920 --> 00:22:26.320
After repelling the imperial cavalry, Bonnivet&nbsp;
took stock of the situation. The enemy artillery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:26.320 --> 00:22:32.320
had run into Fleuranges, who was following a few&nbsp;
hundred meters behind the cavalry, and no further&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:32.320 --> 00:22:39.920
enemy forces were in sight. Convinced the danger&nbsp;
had passed, Bonnivet returned to the king and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:39.920 --> 00:22:46.560
reported that there was nothing to worry about. He&nbsp;
had barely finished speaking when three artillery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:46.560 --> 00:22:54.240
volleys in quick succession proved him wrong.
Fleuranges assumed that the gunfire signaled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:54.240 --> 00:23:01.120
a second wave of attacks and immediately sent a&nbsp;
messenger to the king to raise the alarm. Francis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:01.120 --> 00:23:06.800
now beginning to suspect that something more&nbsp;
serious was unfolding, ordered his men-at-arms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:06.800 --> 00:23:13.520
to prepare. It would take time: according to&nbsp;
contemporary reports, a Gendarme required a good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:13.520 --> 00:23:19.840
half hour to don his heavy armor. As squires in&nbsp;
the French camp helped their masters into greaves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:19.840 --> 00:23:25.360
breastplates, and helmets, Fleuranges and&nbsp;
his Swiss soldiers kept a tense watch over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:25.360 --> 00:23:31.680
the northeastern edge of the park—looking&nbsp;
in precisely the wrong direction. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:31.680 --> 00:23:37.600
the three volleys of artillery were the signal&nbsp;
for the garrison to launch a sortie and open a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:37.600 --> 00:23:45.120
corridor for the arquebusiers carrying supplies.&nbsp;
De Leyva had been waiting tensely for this moment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:45.120 --> 00:23:50.720
As soon as the signal was given, he rushed out&nbsp;
of the city, forced his way through the confused&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:50.720 --> 00:23:57.920
besiegers, and deployed his men in a long double&nbsp;
line stretching from Pavia to the Visconti Park.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:57.920 --> 00:24:03.840
This line secured the route to the city, split&nbsp;
the besieging forces in two, and blocked the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:03.840 --> 00:24:12.160
southern entrance to the park. As a result, the&nbsp;
French army was now divided into three parts.&nbsp;

00:24:12.160 --> 00:24:19.360
A few minutes after the attack began, de Vasto’s&nbsp;
arquebusiers emerged from the forest and quietly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:19.360 --> 00:24:24.720
approached the Castello di Mirabello, the&nbsp;
fortified hunting lodge at the heart of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:24.720 --> 00:24:31.760
Visconti Park. The garrison had no idea that the&nbsp;
enemy was in the park and did not even manage to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:31.760 --> 00:24:37.200
raise the drawbridge before the arquebusiers&nbsp;
stormed the small fortress. They seized the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:37.200 --> 00:24:44.320
Castello and then began looting the nearby camp.&nbsp;
Soon, they became so absorbed in plundering that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:44.320 --> 00:24:52.000
de Vasto could restore order only with difficulty.&nbsp;
He succeeded just in time, because the raid was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:52.000 --> 00:25:02.960
now turning into a battle.
Chapter 5: The Battle&nbsp;

00:25:02.960 --> 00:25:10.080
Shortly after seven, there was movement in the&nbsp;
fog. Fleuranges squinted to see more clearly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:10.080 --> 00:25:17.440
but at first he could only hear it: the stamping&nbsp;
of hundreds of feet, the clatter of weapons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:17.440 --> 00:25:24.400
and low, hushed commands. Then he made out&nbsp;
the silhouette of a single man, then another,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:24.400 --> 00:25:34.480
then a dozen, then thousands. Before him stood&nbsp;
Georg von Frundsberg with 8,000 Landsknechts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:34.480 --> 00:25:40.080
Fortunately for Fleuranges, the poor visibility&nbsp;
meant no one had a clear overview of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:40.080 --> 00:25:47.600
battlefield or the forces they were facing. As&nbsp;
a result, the Landsknechts advanced cautiously.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:47.600 --> 00:25:54.080
Rather than launching a full-scale assault as&nbsp;
was their custom, they applied steady pressure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:54.080 --> 00:26:00.480
using their numerical superiority&nbsp;
to push the Swiss back step by step.&nbsp;

00:26:00.480 --> 00:26:08.160
While Frundsberg engaged the Swiss, the Marquis of&nbsp;
Pescara led 4,000 Landsknechts and 4,000 Spanish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:08.160 --> 00:26:14.800
infantry through the grove toward the Castello&nbsp;
to cover the advance of the arquebusiers. Lannoy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:14.800 --> 00:26:20.880
secured the right flank with 2,000 mounted troops.&nbsp;
The imperial column had just reached the level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:20.880 --> 00:26:28.560
of the French positions when suddenly the fog&nbsp;
lifted. The timing could hardly have been worse:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:28.560 --> 00:26:34.720
they now stood directly in the line of fire of the&nbsp;
French batteries at the Porta Repentita and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:34.720 --> 00:26:41.920
camp. Heavy fire tore through the column before&nbsp;
the soldiers had time to react. Caught completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:41.920 --> 00:26:48.480
by surprise, they attempted to seek cover in&nbsp;
the forest—breaking formation in the process. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:48.480 --> 00:26:56.640
doing so, they exposed themselves to the French&nbsp;
king’s most feared weapon: his heavy cavalry.&nbsp;

00:26:56.640 --> 00:27:02.480
The polished armor of the gendarmes glistened&nbsp;
in the morning light as they streamed onto the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:02.480 --> 00:27:10.320
open plain in front of the camp. In their midst&nbsp;
rode the king. He waited until gaps opened in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:10.320 --> 00:27:18.000
imperial ranks, then gave the order to charge and&nbsp;
pressed his spurs into his horse’s flanks. Moments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:18.000 --> 00:27:24.320
later, the ground trembled under hundreds&nbsp;
of hooves. The French cavalry accelerated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:24.320 --> 00:27:31.200
spreading out as they advanced until they formed&nbsp;
a broad, thin front. Lannoy counterattacked with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:31.200 --> 00:27:38.000
his cavalry, but he stood no chance. The first&nbsp;
French line consisted of the gendarmes—the nobles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:38.000 --> 00:27:44.640
of France on their heavy warhorses. The force&nbsp;
of their charge shattered the imperial line as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:44.640 --> 00:27:47.225
though it were made of glass. Three further lines&nbsp;
of men-at-arms, lighter equipped and more mobile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:47.225 --> 00:27:48.000
followed in succession and launched at the&nbsp;
disintegrating imperial cavalry. Within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:48.000 --> 00:27:54.960
barely ten minutes, Lannoy’s cavalry broke and&nbsp;
fled through the grove. Francis was jubilant.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:54.960 --> 00:28:00.800
Once again, his gendarmes had carried&nbsp;
the day. But Francis had overlooked an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:00.800 --> 00:28:06.720
important shift in military strategy.
The sixteenth century marked the birth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:06.720 --> 00:28:13.520
of pike-and-shot warfare. Heavy cavalry remained&nbsp;
important, but the era in which decisive victories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:13.520 --> 00:28:20.160
were won by cavalry charges alone was coming&nbsp;
to an end. Infantry—composed of pikemen and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:20.160 --> 00:28:27.040
arquebusiers—and artillery were now at least&nbsp;
as crucial. This change was precisely what the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:27.040 --> 00:28:34.640
French king had failed to take into account. Yes,&nbsp;
his gendarmes had defeated the imperial cavalry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:34.640 --> 00:28:40.080
but their charge had also obscured the&nbsp;
French guns, which had to cease firing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:40.080 --> 00:28:46.480
to avoid hitting their comrades. This pause&nbsp;
allowed the imperial infantry to regroup.&nbsp;

00:28:46.480 --> 00:28:53.520
The Marquis of Pescara was an experienced soldier.&nbsp;
He recognized the French mistake and exploited it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:53.520 --> 00:29:00.240
calmly but decisively directing his men to the&nbsp;
edge of the forest north of the Castello. There,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:00.240 --> 00:29:06.640
they formed a long line and opened fire on&nbsp;
the French cavalry. The 3,000 arquebusiers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:06.640 --> 00:29:12.720
of the assault force now also turned against&nbsp;
the gendarmes. They took up positions in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:12.720 --> 00:29:19.200
irrigation ditches near the castle and aimed at&nbsp;
the French flank. This maneuver created a long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:19.200 --> 00:29:26.320
semicircular line of fire that held the French at&nbsp;
bay under a seemingly continuous hail of bullets.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:26.960 --> 00:29:33.360
Suddenly, Francis found himself encircled in the&nbsp;
northeastern part of the park , and suddenly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:33.360 --> 00:29:40.000
Pescara had the opportunity to finish off&nbsp;
the isolated main force of the French army.&nbsp;

00:29:40.000 --> 00:29:47.680
At about half past seven, a panting messenger&nbsp;
reached Georg von Frundsberg. Hastily, he briefed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:47.680 --> 00:29:55.040
him on what had happened west of the grove and&nbsp;
the opportunity that had arisen. Frundsberg needed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:55.040 --> 00:30:01.120
no further explanation. He withdrew half of his&nbsp;
Landsknechts from the fight against the Swiss and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:01.120 --> 00:30:06.480
followed the messenger to support Pescara.&nbsp;
When they reached the edge of the forest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:06.480 --> 00:30:13.760
the battle was already in full swing. The Duke of&nbsp;
Bourbon had rushed onto the field with 4,000 men,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:13.760 --> 00:30:19.760
and the gendarmes were charging repeatedly&nbsp;
against the imperial line. But each time they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:19.760 --> 00:30:25.520
lowered their lances to attack, the arquebusiers&nbsp;
fell back behind the pikemen, and the horsemen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:25.520 --> 00:30:31.920
found themselves driving into an impenetrable&nbsp;
hedge of pikes. The imperial pike-and-shot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:31.920 --> 00:30:37.920
formations were playing to their strengths.
Pavia was the first major battle in which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:37.920 --> 00:30:44.160
sufficient handheld firearms allowed for&nbsp;
a continuous, grueling fire. The French&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:44.160 --> 00:30:51.280
now felt their effects: armor splintered, horses&nbsp;
went down, and men were torn from their saddles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:51.280 --> 00:30:58.480
by the force of the bullets. At close range,&nbsp;
the gendarmes’ armor, though cutting-edge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:58.480 --> 00:31:03.840
offered little protection against the&nbsp;
devastating power of an arquebus shot.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:03.840 --> 00:31:11.520
For the horsemen of France, it was a dark day.
However, the French men-at-arms were not alone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:11.520 --> 00:31:17.280
Francis’s infantry, among them the rest of the&nbsp;
Black Bands, threw themselves against Bourbon’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:17.280 --> 00:31:24.000
contingent and drove it back through the sheer&nbsp;
force of their assault. Then the push of pikes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:24.000 --> 00:31:29.440
began. The men in the front ranks set their&nbsp;
pikes against the enemy with all their strength,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:29.440 --> 00:31:34.560
while those behind pressed forward,&nbsp;
supporting the line from the rear.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:34.560 --> 00:31:41.920
The two formations—each a dense mass of men—pushed&nbsp;
against each other with brute force. Those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:41.920 --> 00:31:48.320
who hesitated endangered the cohesion of the&nbsp;
formation, those who tumbled were trampled and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:48.320 --> 00:31:55.040
those who were slain were immediately replaced.&nbsp;
From time to time, one side managed to force the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:55.040 --> 00:32:01.520
opposing pikes upward. Then, for a brief moment,&nbsp;
small gaps opened, and the most agile men darted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:01.520 --> 00:32:07.760
forward, stabbing at their opponents’ legs before&nbsp;
quickly pulling back, just as the wall of wood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:07.760 --> 00:32:15.170
and iron closed again. There was nothing heroic&nbsp;
about the push of pikes—it was not a spectacle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:15.170 --> 00:32:19.440
It was brutal and relentless, transforming the&nbsp;
fronts of the opposing formations into a ravenous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:19.440 --> 00:32:27.040
man-eating mass of pikes, swords, and daggers.
When Frundsberg saw that Bourbon was in trouble,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:27.040 --> 00:32:32.720
he threw his men into the flank of the&nbsp;
French infantry. Attacked from two sides,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:32.720 --> 00:32:38.560
they stood no chance. The Landsknechts pressed&nbsp;
their advantage and nearly wiped them out to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:38.560 --> 00:32:48.320
last man. Only then did they turn their attention&nbsp;
to the gendarmes—or what remained of them.&nbsp;

00:32:48.320 --> 00:32:54.400
The ruling nobility of France died by the&nbsp;
dozens that day. Most refused to surrender,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:54.400 --> 00:33:02.160
were unhorsed, and cut down. Among the last&nbsp;
to fight was Francis I. Even when his horse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:02.160 --> 00:33:07.360
collapsed beneath him after being struck&nbsp;
by several arquebus rounds, the king did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:07.360 --> 00:33:14.480
not give up. He reportedly fought on until he was&nbsp;
surrounded by enemies, and Lannoy had to turn on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:14.480 --> 00:33:22.000
his own men to save his life. By the time King&nbsp;
Francis was led away, the battle had long been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:22.000 --> 00:33:30.240
decided. The French were in full retreat. In the&nbsp;
northwest, the imperial forces overran their camp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:30.240 --> 00:33:36.800
in the east, the Landsknechts drove the Swiss out&nbsp;
of the park; and near the city, de Leyva launched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:36.800 --> 00:33:43.440
a counterattack. Closely pursued by the garrison&nbsp;
and the Landsknechts, the fleeing French army&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:43.440 --> 00:33:49.760
streamed south and attempted to escape across&nbsp;
a pontoon bridge below Pavia. The improvised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:49.760 --> 00:33:56.320
construction could not withstand the pressure of&nbsp;
the retreat. Only about half of the men made it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:56.320 --> 00:34:03.440
across the Ticino before the bridge collapsed.
When the sun finally broke through the curtain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:03.440 --> 00:34:10.560
of fog, the survivors looked out over a field of&nbsp;
blood and mud. The French had lost around 10,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:10.560 --> 00:34:17.920
men within a few hours, including a large part&nbsp;
of their ruling class. King Francis I was taken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:17.920 --> 00:34:24.400
to Madrid, where he signed a peace treaty—one&nbsp;
he would break just a few months later. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:24.400 --> 00:34:30.640
struggle for supremacy in Europe between&nbsp;
Francis I and Charles V—between the Valois&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:30.640 --> 00:34:37.840
and the Habsburgs—would continue for another&nbsp;
two decades and lead to three further wars.&nbsp;

00:34:37.840 --> 00:34:44.720
The Battle of Pavia did not go according to plan&nbsp;
for either side. Dense fog kept both armies in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:44.720 --> 00:34:50.960
uncertainty for much of the engagement. When&nbsp;
visibility finally improved, the imperial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:50.960 --> 00:34:58.000
forces recognized their opportunity and seized it.&nbsp;
Pavia demonstrated that firearms were the future&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:58.000 --> 00:35:05.360
of warfare and that the new technology possessed&nbsp;
by the infantry could stand up to heavy cavalry.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:05.360 --> 00:35:11.040
In that sense, it marked a symbolic turning&nbsp;
point. No one could ignore the fact that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:11.040 --> 00:35:16.080
imperial infantry had delivered a crushing&nbsp;
defeat to the gendarmes—the most renowned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:16.080 --> 00:35:27.040
men-at-arms in Europe—that foggy morning.&nbsp;
The age of pike-and-shot warfare had begun.

